WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.070
<v Speaker>So, now let's talk about placement groups.</v>

2
00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.840
Placement groups are a little bit more advanced,

3
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.780
and we want to use them once we want to have control over

4
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.810
how our EC2 instances are going to be placed

5
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.510
within the AWS infrastructure.

6
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.130
So, that strategy can be defined

7
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.990
using these placement groups.

8
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.330
So, we don't get direct interaction

9
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.950
with the hardware of AWS,

10
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.157
but we let AWS know how we would like our EC2 instance

11
00:00:23.157 --> 00:00:25.890
to be placed compared to one another.

12
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.750
So, when you create a placement group,

13
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.480
you have three strategies available for you.

14
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.040
You have the cluster placement group,

15
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.920
in which your instances will be grouped together

16
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.770
in a low latency hardware setup

17
00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.723
within a single availability zone.

18
00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:42.270
This is going to give you high performance, but high risk.

19
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.190
We'll see this in details in a second.

20
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.410
Spread means that your instances

21
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.590
are going to be spread across different hardware.

22
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.000
And that is a restriction on this

23
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.670
that means you can only have seven EC2 instance

24
00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.850
per placement group that's spread per AZ.

25
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.250
So, you would use a spread placement group

26
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.650
when you have critical applications.

27
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.050
Finally, the last one is a new kind of placement group

28
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.190
that is really helpful.

29
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.510
It's called partition.

30
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.400
It's similar to the spread,

31
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.800
meaning that you want to spread your instances,

32
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.650
but here they're spread across many different partitions.

33
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.360
And these partitions rely on

34
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.080
different sets of racks of hardware within an AZ.

35
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.150
What does that mean is that they're still spread,

36
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.970
but they're not isolated one from another failure,

37
00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.920
but a partition should be isolated from

38
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.840
another partition of failure.

39
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.910
The idea with this is that you can scale to hundreds

40
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.100
of EZ2 instances per group

41
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.050
and that allows you to run applications

42
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.690
such as Hadoop, Cassandra, or Kafka.

43
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.220
Finally, you have precision time,

44
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.300
which gives you direct access

45
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.193
to high precision time sources.

46
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.560
Now let's have a look into

47
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.470
each of these placement groups in details.

48
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.920
So next we have the cluster placement group

49
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.410
and all your EC2 instances are going

50
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.800
to be in the same availability zone.

51
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.360
So why do we do this?

52
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.450
Well, this particular placement group

53
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.520
gives you great networking.

54
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.230
So you have around 10 gigabits per second of bandwidth

55
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.090
between all these instances

56
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.730
that will have enhanced networking enabled.

57
00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.210
And so you get low latency, high throughput network,

58
00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.830
and the idea is that out of this

59
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.970
you can get great performance

60
00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.640
for any kind of computational job.

61
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.120
So a drawback of using this architecture is

62
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.730
that if your availability zone fails,

63
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.910
then all the instances will fail at the same time.

64
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.190
But usually for use case, this is fine.

65
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.531
So the use cases for this kind of placement group

66
00:02:34.531 --> 00:02:38.282
are big data jobs that need to complete very fast

67
00:02:38.282 --> 00:02:40.740
with very high networking

68
00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.710
or applications that need extremely low latency

69
00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.240
and high throughput network

70
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.230
between each instance of your application.

71
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.030
Now spread is the complete opposite

72
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.700
and spread want to minimize the failure risk.

73
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.240
And so in this case, when we ask for spread placement group,

74
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.830
all the EC2 instances

75
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.770
are going to be located on different hardware.

76
00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.490
So you can see here we have three AZ and we have six EC2

77
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.940
and each EZ2 instance is on a different hardware.

78
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.020
So what does that mean?

79
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.140
Well, what we get is that we can span across multiple AZ

80
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.320
and there is a reduced risk of simultaneous failure.

81
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.620
Why? Well, because if my hardware one fails,

82
00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.990
I'm pretty sure my hardware two will not fail.

83
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.610
And so we've separated the risk

84
00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.320
of my two instances in the USES 1A to fail at the same time.

85
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.030
And so that's the benefit from it.

86
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.910
The con is that from this configuration,

87
00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.720
we're limited to seven instances per AZ per placement group.

88
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.510
So there is a limit to how big your placement group can be

89
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.340
and so you need to have an application

90
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.460
that's going to be of good size but not too big.

91
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.050
The use case would be an application

92
00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.500
that needs to maximize high availability and reduce the risk

93
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.170
and in general for critical applications

94
00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.760
where your instance failures

95
00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.250
must be isolated from one another.

96
00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.380
Remember here we have a limitation

97
00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.280
of seven instances per AZ per placement group.

98
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.440
Now for the partition placement group,

99
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.740
we can have instances spread across partitions in

100
00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.690
multiple availability zones.

101
00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.180
So we can have up to seven partitions per AZ.

102
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.710
So in this example, we have partition one

103
00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.920
and partition two in US East 1A

104
00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.070
and partition three in US East 1B.

105
00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.070
And on each partition you can have many EC2 instances.

106
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.540
So in the first one I have four

107
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.590
and the second one I have four

108
00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.780
and the third one have four as well.

109
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.760
So why do we use a partition placement group?

110
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.540
Well, each partition represents a rack in AWS.

111
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.860
And so by having many partitions,

112
00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.870
you're making sure that your instances

113
00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.540
are distributed across many hardware racks

114
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.990
and so therefore they're safe from a rack failure

115
00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:43.823
from one another.

116
00:04:43.823 --> 00:04:46.740
So you can have up to seven partitions per AZ

117
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.200
and these partitions can span across

118
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.950
multiple availability zones in the same region.

119
00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.460
You can get up to hundreds of EC2 instances with a setup.

120
00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:57.540
So this is a difference

121
00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.360
versus the spread type of placement group.

122
00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.270
And as I said, the instances in the partition

123
00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.894
do not share the same hardware physical rack

124
00:05:05.894 --> 00:05:08.610
with the instances in the other partitions

125
00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.740
and therefore each partition is isolated from failure.

126
00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:14.940
So that means that yes, if one goes down,

127
00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.580
if partition goes down the partition number two,

128
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.130
then partition number one should be fine.

129
00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:24.810
And to know which partition these EC2 instances are on,

130
00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.930
there is an option to access this information

131
00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:31.203
as with using the metadata service.

132
00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:33.840
So when would you use a partition placement group?

133
00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:35.340
Well, when you have an application

134
00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.310
that it can be partition aware to distribute the data

135
00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.320
and your servers across partitions

136
00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.510
and usually the use cases are going to be

137
00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:46.140
big data applications which are partition aware

138
00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:51.140
such as using HDFS, HBase, Cassandra and Apache Kafka.

139
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.180
So for the precision time placement group,

140
00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.150
the idea is that you want to place your EC2 instances

141
00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.300
on underlying hardware with direct access to

142
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.280
high precision time sources.

143
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.113
What does that mean?

144
00:06:03.113 --> 00:06:05.520
That means that the clock of your underlying Linux,

145
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.620
for example, will be connected

146
00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.780
to the Amazon time sync service,

147
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.490
which is going to give you a time

148
00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:15.030
that is much more accurate than the standard NTP.

149
00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.910
Also, you have access to a PTP hardware clock

150
00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.869
and hardware packet timestamping.

151
00:06:19.869 --> 00:06:24.690
So the idea is that you have very, very, very precise time

152
00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.340
on these instances,

153
00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.340
which is good only for some very specific applications

154
00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.800
such as distributed databases, financial timestamping

155
00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.480
or event ordering.

156
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.610
For web and that kind of stuff,

157
00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.920
the time you get on all these machines is good enough.

158
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.440
But if you have a time constraint,

159
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.740
then you can use the precision type placement group

160
00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.090
to get placed on the hardware

161
00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.940
that supports this more accurate time.

162
00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.290
So that's it for this lecture.

163
00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:55.323
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

